  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 124 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nBy Sunday, most locations will once again see highs in the triple \ndigits as the center of the upper ridge settles overhead. Sunday may \nalso see the return of heat related headlines. Low level \ntemperatures do not warm as much as the last couple of weeks, so \neven though the area will see triple digit highs, currently do not \nanticipate the extreme temperatures that were seen this last week. \nIn addition, the upper ridge continues to translate eastward, so by \nearly next week the center of the ridge moves toward the eastern \npart of the country. Unfortunately, the ridge will continue to have \njust enough influence over the southern Plains to keep most of the \narea hot and dry. One exception may be northwest Oklahoma, where \nproximity to the southwest flow may allow a few showers/storms to \noccur.\n\nWinds are expected to increase as well Sunday and Monday as a trough \nsets up across the west with embedded shortwaves ejecting out of the \nsouthwest into the central and northern Plains. This will cause lee \nsurface trough to develop aiding in increasing pressure gradient \nthrough the Plains. By Tuesday, the upper ridge once again expands \nthrough the central and southern Plains, which will push any \nshortwave troughs farther north away from the area, which should \nallow winds to relax to some degree. This will also limit any \npossibility of precip during this time frame.",
